 one shot the british government had  promised to give 15 million people the  vaccine by monday  prime minister boris johnson says he's  optimistic kova 19 lockdown measures  could be eased soon  coming up the deadly consequences of  fossil fuels and what the world  needs to do about it  it's long been known that pollution from  burning fossil fuels is extremely  harmful to our health  a new report paints a much darker  picture it  estimates more than 8 million people die  every year  after breathing the emissions that are  left in the air as redmond shannon  reports  experts believe making the move to  renewable energy is more critical  than ever before  air pollution is a huge health issue  particularly in rapidly developing  nations  the most recent estimates had said 4  million people die  every year due to the microscopic pm  2.5 particles released when burning  fossil fuels and  biomass but now researchers led by  harvard university say it could be more  than  double that number even when the effects  of forest fires wood burning and dust  are excluded we estimate just from  fossil fuel combustion  that there are 8.7 million premature  deaths  each year that means almost one in five  adult premature deaths worldwide could  be down to pollution coming from burning  coal oil and gas scientists used  existing data that links emissions and  disease  and then added a mathematical model that  divided the world into smaller segments  it helped identify how dense pockets of  pollution  can affect deaths and so what we could  do with the simulation is tease out the  contribution from fossil fuel combustion  and so when we combined those two tools  we estimated premature mortality that's  much greater than  previously reported more than half the  deaths are in china and  india though both countries are taking  measures to reduce  pollution the report estimates that more  than 13 percent of premature adult  deaths in canada were caused by fossil  fuel pollution in 2018.  broken down by province ontario was the  highest at more than  17 newfoundland and labrador was less  than two percent  however the data they used for canada is  already somewhat outdated  and there is a wide margin of error but  the results are still  broadly in line with what canadian  experts have seen  what some of these newer studies are  finding is that even at relatively  low concentrations relatively good air  we still  see a significant impact in terms of  increased risk to our health that risk  caused by pollutants is on top of the  impacts of climate change  the drive to net zero must become the  new normal for everyone everywhere  for every country every company city  financial institution  the un's crucial cop 26 climate change  summit is scheduled for november in  glasgow  when each nation is expected to present  more ambitious targets  for reducing emissions redmond shannon  global news  london still ahead myanmar's  pro-democracy protests enter a second  week but will the military  relent  building shook in japan when a 7.1  magnitude earthquake struck off the  country's northeastern coastline  the quake hit just after 11 pm local  time near the site of the fukushima  nuclear disaster  there were widespread power outages and  dozens of people were injured  the tremors were felt hundreds of  kilometers away in the capital tokyo  defiant protests against the military  coup in myanmar have entered a second  week  hundreds of demonstrators gathered  outside the u.s and chinese embassies in  yangon  the country's largest city demanding the  release of aung san tsuchi  thousands more took to the streets in  the capital earlier this month the  military seized power ending half a  decade of democracy  under sushi  protests initially planned to show  support for myanmar's pro-democracy  movement  in thailand quickly morphed into wild  demonstrations against laws  that punish people for mocking the king  scuffles broke out as protesters pushed  for the release of four activists  arrested for insulting thailand's king  a crime that amounts to 15 years in  prison  up next the academy supporting black  talent in canada  often canadians have to make it in the  u.s before their talent is recognized  and celebrated here at home  that problem is significantly worse for  black creatives in this country  a lack of support and opportunities in  canada has led to a gap in black  representation in the arts  as kayla mclean explains it's why two  canadian brothers are stepping up to  help  the next generation gentlemen right here  together  they starred in big box office dramas  and garnered award show recognition  please join me in honoring my little  brother  but for toronto raised actors and  brothers to fond james and shanir  anderson  their hollywood success is tempered by a  deep sense of responsibility to change  the industry  at home four or five years ago you know  shamir and i were seeing much of the  same problems that we see today  um you know in terms of just there being  a lack of support  and celebration and recognition of you  know black artists doing incredible  things  the lack of opportunity support and  visibility for black creatives in this  country sparked the brothers next act  the black academy a first of its kind in  canada  and a way to foster and showcase black  talent there's not enough  governing bodies infrastructures places  to celebrate black excellence  i mean when black people slip and fall  we see that all over the news  it's very rare we get to hear the  pinball clemens of the world we're doing  incredible stuff  very little data exists on black  creators both behind  and on the screen in canada but the  stats that do exist paint a troubling  picture  racialized and black communities make up  about 22 percent of the population in  this country  but according to the black screen office  in the history of television  broadcasting  only five shows led by black creatives  have made it onto canadian television  for longtime canadian writer and  producer jennifer holness the stark  disparity means a huge cultural loss and  perpetuation of systemic racism in the  canadian industry  think about all we have lost  think about the stories that haven't  been told  think about the people with the talent  that we haven't seen shamir and stefan  hope to combat those inequities by  bringing black talent to the fore in  canada  but much needed financial backing for  the black academy is proving to be  elusive  outside of the canada media fund no  other organization has stepped up to  pledge their financial support  something shamir is calling on the  government and corporate canada  to change fostering talent but not just  you know a one-time check that's cut  from the government because someone  feels bad today  you know it's not that it's  sustainability it's permanent  kayla mclean global news to learn more  about how the brothers hope to make real  and lasting change  watch the new reality with donna friesen  tonight right here on global and that is  global national for this saturday night  i'm robin gill we leave you with a rare  sight in vancouver bc  it's snowing the city woke up to a  winter wonderland  thank you for watching we'll see you  back here tomorrow have a great night  [Music]
 

